arXiv:2509.15339v1 [cs.CL] 18 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge 
<br class="ltx_break">in Large Language Models</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yeongbin Seo                  
Dongha Lee                   
Jinyoung Yeo
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Artificial Intelligence
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Yonsei University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{suhcrates,donalee,jinyeo}@yonsei.ac.kr</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Hallucination prediction in large language models (LLMs) is often interpreted as a sign of self-awareness. However, we argue that such performance can arise from question-side shortcuts rather than true model-side introspection. To disentangle these factors, we propose the Approximate Question-side Effect (AQE), which quantifies the contribution of question-awareness. Our analysis across multiple datasets reveals that much of the reported success stems from exploiting superficial patterns in questions. We further introduce SCAO (Semantic Compression by Answering in One word), a method that enhances the use of model-side signals. Experiments show that SCAO achieves strong and consistent performance, particularly in settings with reduced question-side cues, highlighting its effectiveness in fostering genuine self-awareness in LLMs. The code is available online (<a href="https://github.com/ybseo-ac/AQE" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/ybseo-ac/AQE</a>).</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The defining and quantifying of the human-like mental attributes of large language models (LLMs) lie at the heart of a long-standing human curiosity: whether artificial systems can possess minds akin to our own. While the field has demonstrated that LLMs can rival or even surpass human-level performance in rational reasoning (e.g., reading, solving a logical problem) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, higher-order human-like traits (e.g., self-awareness, personality, emotion) remain poorly understood, despite growing interest <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, partly due to the inherent ambiguity in their definition and measurement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Among higher-order attributes, <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">self-awareness of knowledge</span> has been considered as a key topic, given its strong association with hallucination detection—a critical factor in ensuring the reliability of LLMs. While hallucination arises from various causes (e.g., failure of reading, reasoning), it is well known that a major cause is when the model is asked questions on the knowledge not pre-trained <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. Humans possess an innate ability of self-awareness to recognize the possession of their knowledge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, and thus refuse to answer when they lack the relevant knowledge. In contrast, LLMs lack such self-awareness and therefore tend to produce any probable output even when they lack knowledge, which leads to hallucination. In this respect, self-awareness is a fundamental component in achieving reliable language generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then, how can we define and measure self-awareness of LLMs?
Prior work has often equated self-awareness with the behaviour of hallucination detection itself, perhaps because it is the ultimate practical goal we expect from self-awareness. Indeed, recent studies have reported surprisingly high precision on hallucination detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, raising hopes that self-awareness in LLMs may be within reach.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, in this work, we argue that hallucination prediction (i.e., early detection) does not equate to self-awareness. This is because two distinct sources of information are typically involved in the prediction process: (1) information about the model itself and (2) information about the question. As such, hallucination prediction reflects a mixture of <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">model-awareness (self-awareness)</span> and <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">question-awareness</span>. To isolate the true contribution of self-awareness, we propose to disentangle these two components. Through this separation, we offer a rigorous and measurable definition of self-awareness, which we believe serves as a foundational step toward investigating the higher-order mental faculties of LLMs. To enable this separation, we introduce a metric to quantify the question-awareness in hallucination detection, the <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>pproximate <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q</span>uestion-side <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E</span>ffect (AQE). The contribution of self-awareness is estimated by subtracting AQE from hallucination detection precision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Quantifying the contribution of self-awareness to hallucination prediction performance is not merely a matter of curiosity: it carries significant practical implications, in our observation in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Approximate question-side effect ‣ 3 AQE: assessing question-side effects of hallucination prediction datasets ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§3.2</span></a>. When hallucination prediction relies on question-awareness, models tend to exploit dataset-specific shortcuts to easily achieve high performance. However, such performance fails to generalize when the distribution of questions shifts even slightly. In contrast, relying on self-awareness tends to produce more consistent results across varying question distributions, as it utilizes model-side information rather than question-side cues. To support this claim, in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Other question-side shortcuts ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§B</span></a>, we first analyze dataset-specific shortcut patterns in existing datasets used in previous hallucination detection works. Then we provide the following empirical findings in <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Measuring AQE across datasets ‣ 3 AQE: assessing question-side effects of hallucination prediction datasets ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§3.3</span></a> and <a href="#S5" title="5 Experiment on hallucination prediction approaches ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§5</span></a>: (1) Datasets containing shortcut cues tend to exhibit higher AQE scores, suggesting stronger question-awareness signals. However, AQE scores drop significantly under shortcut-controlled settings (e.g., out-of-domain scenarios). (2) In such high-AQE datasets, hallucination detection performance shows limited generalizability</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Lastly, we also propose a method to enhance the use of model-side information, by leveraging the confidence scores of LLMs more effectively. The proposed method is <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>emantic <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>ompression by <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>nswering in <span id="S1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">O</span>ne word (SCAO). We demonstrate that SCAO performs particularly well in low AQE settings, and the performance is consistent across varying settings of questions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a measurable definition of “self-awareness” in LLMs, distinct from question-awareness, and introduce AQE to quantify its contribution. This offers a concrete example for studying human-like mental attributes in language models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze existing hallucination prediction datasets and reveal that much of the reported performance stems from question-side shortcuts, limiting generalizability.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce SCAO, a simple one-word answering method that enhances model-side signal utilization, showing strong performance particularly when question-side shortcuts are unavailable and self-awareness becomes essential.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Definition</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first examine the definition of self-awareness of human. Next, we define the task formulation of hallucination prediction, to establish a definition of self-awareness in LLMs. And we review the definitions from previous works. More detailed review on the related works of neuropsychology and LLMs is in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Related works ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition of self-awareness of human    </span>
In psychology, human self-awareness is defined as the capability of perception of one’s own mental processes or states, which includes thoughts, feelings, emotions, and knowing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. In this work, we focus on the self-awareness of knowing certain pieces of knowledge, which is also referred to as self-knowledge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Some studies on LLMs also borrow the term “meta-cognition” from psychology to refer to self-knowledge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, which may be inaccurate. It is because meta-cognition focuses solely on the conscious level <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, while recent cognitive neuroscience research suggests that the human brain conducts judgment of knowing even at the unconscious level <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, which is even before consciously recognizing the meaning of the question.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition of hallucination prediction   </span>
The term “hallucination” has been broadly used to refer to the phenomenon where a model provides an incorrect answer to a given question <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Thus, “hallucination detection” refers to the task of predicting whether a response is incorrect <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we focus specifically on (1) hallucination from <span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">factoid questions</span> that examine whether the model possesses certain knowledge, as this has been widely used as a clear and straightforward scenario for exploring hallucination detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>. (2) And we focus on an early detection (i.e., <span id="S2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">prediction</span>) scenario <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> where <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is predicted before answer generation, as this setting is more appropriate for examine self-awareness (we describe this in the next section).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To formulate the common problem setting of hallucination prediction, let <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> represent the model, <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> the query, and <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> the answer label. The model <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> infers <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> from the input <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. The correctness of <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> can then be measured by evaluating its similarity to <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="S2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, representing a binary value (True/False). Common evaluation methods include string matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, GLUE, and G-eval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. During this process, a datapoint <math id="S2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta,x}=\theta(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta,x}=\theta(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is extracted from <math id="S2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, which contains information about how <math id="S2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> perceived <math id="S2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote this as <math id="S2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> for simplicity. Through a series of question-answering and evaluation processes, we obtain a dataset <math id="S2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(s_{i},k_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(s_{i},k_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the dataset size. From this, hallucination prediction is defined as a binary classification task where a learnable module <math id="S2.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> learn to take input <math id="S2.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to predict <math id="S2.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we note <math id="S2.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k_{i}}=\phi(s_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k_{i}}=\phi(s_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> as a transformer model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> can be mainly two forms (1) Hidden state vectors: Transformer models are composed of multiple attention layers, where each layer passes a fixed-size vector (i.e., hidden states) to the next. These vectors encode the semantic and contextual information of the input <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, and are also known to contain information about the model’s response that will be generated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, providing a cue for hallucination prediction.
(2) Confidence score: This refers to the softmax probability that the causal LLMs predict for the next token generation. While the hidden state is a high-dimensional representation (4096 dimensions in <span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaMA-3-8B</span>), the confidence score is a scalar value. As the hidden state contains richer information, it has achieved the best performance as a source for hallucination prediction and has been regarded as the main source <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Formulating the self-awareness of LLM</h3>

<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15339v1/images/flow_of_information.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/70;" width="429" height="70" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pipeline for the prediction of knowing (prediction of hallucination). </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We defined hallucination prediction as the process where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> perceives <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> to infer <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to predict <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> represents the state of the model after it has seen a question, it inherently contains information of two distinct objects, the question-side and the model-side, as illustrated in <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.1 Formulating the self-awareness of LLM ‣ 2 Definition ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 1</span></a>. The question-side refers to the objective information that can be shared across different models, such as the domain of question (e.g., science, math, society) and the type of question (e.g., multiple choice, open-ended). For human, this type of information is derived from rational abilities (e.g., classification and reading comprehension), rather than from higher-order mental capacity such as self-awareness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. In contrast, the model-side information refers to model-specific attributes, such as the possession of the knowledge that is needed for response, or the degree of confidence for answering. In humans, this corresponds to the domain of self-awareness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let us denote the representation of the question-side information in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. If we exclude <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, what remains is the information specific to the model <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Simplifying this, we can rewrite this as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\approx s_{Q}\oplus s_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\approx s_{Q}\oplus s_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dim hidden state. By definition, we assume that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is the question-side information that is independent of model-side information (<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>), and thus the two share no overlap. For example, the domain and type of a question remain invariant regardless of the state of the model. Therefore, the previously defined hallucination prediction <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}=\phi(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}=\phi(s)</annotation></semantics></math> can be rewritten as: <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}=\phi(s_{Q},s_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}=\phi(s_{Q},s_{M})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We do not claim that the hidden state <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is perfectly linearly separable. However, we adopt this assumption based on the following intuition: Prior studies have empirically shown that the hidden states of transformer models encode multiple properties in a linearly separable manner. For instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. [19]</a></cite> demonstrates that the hidden state contains a direction associated with the attribute of “truthfulness”, therefore linearly adding this to the hidden states results in more truthful responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> learns to predict <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> using two sources of information (<math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Q},s_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Q},s_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>), utilizing question-side information can be regarded as question-awareness, while utilizing model-side information can be regarded as model-awareness, which is “self-awareness” in the perspective of model.
Thus, self-awareness can be formally expressed as:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{k}=\phi(s_{M})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{k}=\phi(s_{M}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why hallucination prediction, not detection?   </span>
We argue that hallucination prediction is a more suitable setting than hallucination detection for examining self-awareness. In hallucination detection, <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> perceives model-generated answers when predicting <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be formulated as <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}=\phi(s_{M},s_{Q},x,\hat{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}=\phi(s_{M},s_{Q},x,\hat{y})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is the question and <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is the generated answer. As self-awareness is defined as <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}=\phi(s_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}=\phi(s_{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, additional inputs <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> serve as distracting factors, making it difficult to isolate the effect of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Intuitively, this detection scenario may become more of a reading comprehension task over <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than assessing the model’s internal states. Therefore, we choose
hallucination prediction scenario to more clearly examine the effect of self-awareness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Definition from previous works</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Utilizing <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> as self-awareness  </span>
Previous works implicitly regard self-awareness as the act of hallucination detection itself. In other words, the focus has been on the act of predicting <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, with no consideration given to the decomposition of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. As a result, some of the hallucination detection performance reported in those works is partially overestimated by the effect of question-side shortcuts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. We analyze such cases in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Case study on question-side shortcuts ‣ 3 AQE: assessing question-side effects of hallucination prediction datasets ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Utilizing <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> as self-awareness   </span>
In another case, some works define utilization of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> as self-awareness, which is the opposite concept from our view. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> defines the term “self-knowledge” as a self-awareness on possession of certain knowledge. And from this definition, they construct the dataset SelfAware to measure the self-awareness capacity. While the definition in the paper is consistent with ours, the construction of the dataset stands on the opposite definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The dataset SelfAware consists of “answerable” and “unanswerable” questions, and the capacity of self-awareness is defined as the classification between the two. An unanswerable question refers to one that is philosophical (e.g., "What is a happy life?") or subjective (“Do you like to go to the mountains?”), where no definitive answer can be given, thus inevitably leading to hallucination. This setting is contradicting the term “self-awareness” in three points: (1) the unanswerability defined in the SelfAware involves no model-side information. For example, the question “Do you like to go to the mountains?” is always classified as “unanswerable” in SelfAware, regardless of how much knowledge about mountains is stored in the answerer (e.g., 1B LM, 70B LM, or a human).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">(2) The dataset includes fixed labels indicating the unanswerability of each question, therefore unanswerability is entirely independent from the model’s state. As unanswerability represents whether the model can answer a given question, it aligns with <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in our problem setting (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 Formulating the self-awareness of LLM ‣ 2 Definition ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation 1</span></a>). However, as <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in SelfAware is determined entirely by properties of the question and not the model’s state, the task proposed in this dataset becomes <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\phi(s_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\phi(s_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, excluding <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. This problem setting is a question-awareness, not a self-awareness, from our definition. (3) For humans, the ability needed for this classification task is reading comprehension, which is not self-awareness.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>AQE: assessing question-side effects of hallucination prediction datasets</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As question-awareness is using and relying on the question-side information, in this section, we first identify the data-specific shortcuts that cause dependency on the question-side information, through a case study in existing datasets for hallucination prediction. Next, we introduce our novel metric AQE, a method for quantifying the effect of question-side shortcuts in that dataset.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Case study on question-side shortcuts</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate sources of question-side shortcuts in datasets used in hallucination prediction studies. We focus on short-form closed-book factoid question answering scenarios, which include ParaRel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, Mintaka <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, HaluEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, HotpotQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, and SimpleQuestion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. We describe three main sources of question-side shortcuts, and further in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Other question-side shortcuts ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Broken question   </span>
The most commonly observed problem across all datasets is insufficient annotations of question and answer pairs. This occurs when questions and answers follow a one-to-many relationship, but the annotations fail to cover them. For example, the ParaRel consists of question-answer pairs such as: “Q: What field does Daniel Bernoulli work in? A: physics”. Although Daniel Bernoulli also worked in the fields of mathematics and medicine (one-to-many relation), only one label is provided, failing to cover all possible correct answers. If an LLM responds with a correct answer but different from the given label (e.g., mathematics), it would still be classified as hallucinated (i.e., <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is annotated <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="False" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">False</annotation></semantics></math>), which is incorrect. If <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is trained to predict <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> using such <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s,k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s,k\}</annotation></semantics></math> pairs, it will likely become biased toward predicting <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}=False" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}=False</annotation></semantics></math> whenever the domain of question is “field of work”. This means <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> learns a domain classification task, not self-awareness. While this may improve prediction performance within the dataset, it lacks generalizability. It is because its performance is likely to drop only if the quality of the dataset improves, or if questions are given from unseen domains. As a study of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. [42]</a></cite> reports hallucination performance on this dataset, this shortcut might have influenced the reported scores. This issue is also found in other datasets such as SimpleQuestions, which includes instances like "Q: What is a TV action show? A: Genji Tsushin Agedama", that also fail to cover various possible answers.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:39%;"><img src="2509.15339v1/images/pararel_true_rate.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:172/145;" width="172" height="145" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The portion of <math id="S3.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=True" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=True</annotation></semantics></math> for each domain, by <span id="S3.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaMA-3-8B</span> model on the ParaRel train set. The rate is skewed toward 0 or 1 by domain, rather than being centered around 0.5.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The broken question problem is addressed when a question includes detailed constraints that restrict the one-to-many mapping between the question and possible answers. For example, in Mintaka, detailed constraints are added to the questions to ensure a one-to-one mapping (e.g., “Who was the director of The Goodfellas and attended school at New York University’s School of Film?”).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Domain   </span>
Classifying the domain of a question (e.g., science, society) alone can provide a rough guess of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, suppose a model that is extensively trained on science data but is unfamiliar with society or history domains. Since the model is likely to make more hallucination when given questions in the history domain. In this way, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> can be biased toward <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="True" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">True</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="False" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">False</annotation></semantics></math> by domains, as shown in <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Case study on question-side shortcuts ‣ 3 AQE: assessing question-side effects of hallucination prediction datasets ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 2</span></a>. In such cases, the task of predicting <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is more a domain classification, which is question-aware and not model-aware.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1.st2" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:19%;">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.st2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.T1.st2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The portion of correct answer for each question type, by <span id="S3.T1.st2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaMA-3-8B</span>. The rate for binary type is in <span id="S3.T1.st2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>.</span></figcaption>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.st2.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.T1.st2.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">HotpotQA</span></figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S3.T1.st2.9" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:69.0pt;height:18.7pt;vertical-align:-8.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-34.0pt,9.2pt) scale(0.503501135682522,0.503501135682522) ;">
<table id="S3.T1.st2.9.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T1.st2.9.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.st2.9.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Type</td>
<td id="S3.T1.st2.9.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.st2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(k=True)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(k=True)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.st2.9.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.st2.9.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Bridge</td>
<td id="S3.T1.st2.9.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.6828</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.st2.9.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.st2.9.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">Comparison</td>
<td id="S3.T1.st2.9.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.st2.9.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.8477</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.st2.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.T1.st2.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Mintaka</span></figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S3.T1.st2.12" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:69.0pt;height:39.7pt;vertical-align:-18.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-29.1pt,16.8pt) scale(0.542186681668806,0.542186681668806) ;">
<table id="S3.T1.st2.12.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Type</td>
<td id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.st2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(k=True)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(k=True)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Entity</td>
<td id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.5508</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">numerical</td>
<td id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.4011</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">date</td>
<td id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.5968</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">string</td>
<td id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.6315</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">boolean</td>
<td id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.st2.12.1.6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.7283</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">When we consider a case of a human, it becomes clear that such shortcuts have limited effectiveness and are far from self-aware. For example, suppose this model is not familiar with the history domain but still has knowledge about Abraham Lincoln. If this model were human, he could easily “feel” his inner state and recognize that he possesses knowledge about Lincoln, despite not being familiar with other historical issues. However, if the model were an LLM and <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is trained to exploit only question-side shortcuts, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> would tell the model does not know about Lincoln, though the model actually possesses the knowledge of Lincoln. Therefore, while the domain information of a question can provide a naive approximation of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, relying on it imposes limitations on precision. This again highlights that utilizing self-awareness is the ultimate direction in precise hallucination prediction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Question type   </span>
Question type (e.g., short-answer, multiple-choice) also provides a strong hint for predicting <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. The average probability of <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=True" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=True</annotation></semantics></math> (denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(k=True)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(k=True)</annotation></semantics></math>) for binary-choice questions is at least 0.5 for random choice, significantly higher than for open-ended questions with 0 for random choice, as described in <a href="#S3.T1.st2" title="Table 1(b) ‣ 3.1 Case study on question-side shortcuts ‣ 3 AQE: assessing question-side effects of hallucination prediction datasets ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>. In such a case, <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> may learn a shortcut where it automatically predicts <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=True" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=True</annotation></semantics></math> whenever it detects a binary-choice question. HotpotQA, HaluEval, and Mintaka contain such binary-choice questions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">There are various other question-side shortcuts, which are described in the <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Other question-side shortcuts ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§B</span></a>. These shortcuts can be identified by considering various scenarios in which they may act as shortcuts, and it is likely that some shortcuts remain undiscovered, as it is very subtle to determine. Therefore, manually identifying and removing them from datasets is nontrivial. That is why we introduce AQE in the next section, a method for approximately assessing the total effect of question-side shortcuts without human investigation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Approximate question-side effect</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe the concept of AQE.
Repeating <a href="#S2" title="2 Definition ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§2</span></a>, the model <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is given a question <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to generate answer, where the correctness is denoted as a binary representation <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. A hidden state <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is extracted at the first position of the answer. And <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is consist of model-side <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and question-side information <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Learnable module <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> can perceive <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> to infer <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to predict <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, which is hallucination prediction. Self-awareness is defined as utilizing <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to predict <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, what we ultimately aim to measure is the effect of utilizing <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> in predicting <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{M}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{M}))</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a metric that measures the correctness of the predicted <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. As all we can measure is <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{Q},s_{M}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{Q},s_{M}))</annotation></semantics></math>, we decompose this as follows: <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{Q},s_{M}))\approx\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{Q}))+\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{M}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{Q},s_{M}))\approx\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{Q}))+\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{M}))</annotation></semantics></math>
This allows us to measure the effect of self-awareness as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{M}))\approx\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{Q},s_{M}))-\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{Q}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{M}))\approx\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{Q},s_{M}))-\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{Q}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">AQE as a Shapley analysis</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To provide a clearer theoretical ground, we formalize AQE as a special case of Shapley analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">fryer2021shapley</span>]</cite>, which evaluates the impact of individual factors on the outcome. Specifically, AQE corresponds to the concept of <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">marginal contribution</span>—a metric that quantifies the separate contriubtion of a single factor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Gamma_{\beta}(\alpha)=\Gamma(\alpha\cup\beta)-\Gamma(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Gamma_{\beta}(\alpha)=\Gamma(\alpha\cup\beta)-\Gamma(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ AQE as a Shapley analysis ‣ 3.2 Approximate question-side effect ‣ 3 AQE: assessing question-side effects of hallucination prediction datasets ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation 3</span></a>is the general formulation of marginal contribution. Here, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> represent individual components of a system. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\cup\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\cup\beta</annotation></semantics></math> represents a state in which these components are mixed together. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the baseline performance metric (e.g., AUROC). <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\beta}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\beta}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the impact of removing <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, thus isolating the contribution of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. In our setting, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to the model-side and question-side information, respectively. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\cup\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\cup\beta</annotation></semantics></math> can be interpreted as the information contained in the hidden state of the model, which integrates both the model and question side information. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\alpha\cup\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\alpha\cup\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{\beta}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{\beta}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\phi(s))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\phi(s))</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\phi(s_{Q}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\phi(s_{Q}))</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\phi(s_{M}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\phi(s_{M}))</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Computing of self-awareness</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Computing <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\phi(s_{Q}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\phi(s_{Q}))</annotation></semantics></math> is achieved by introducing a distinct model <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}\neq\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}\neq\theta</annotation></semantics></math>) which is optimized to only embed basic properties of the input question (e.g., domain or question type), <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}_{Q}\approx s^{\prime}=\theta^{\prime}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}_{Q}\approx s^{\prime}=\theta^{\prime}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. A representative example of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is sBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. sBERT is a very small model with only 22.7M parameters, but it is optimized to generate an embedding vector <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> from input text <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., question). sBERT is known to capture high-level information from text as effectively as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> with a larger architecture (e.g., <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaMA-3-8B</span>), as long as the target information is simple enough (e.g., domain classification). Therefore, while <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Q}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Q}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> reside in different representational spaces of two distinct models, they are assumed to capture similar high-level information (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Q}^{\prime}\sim s_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Q}^{\prime}\sim s_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>).
Conversely, due to its small size, we can assume <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> holds a very small amount of knowledge, which makes the knowledge distribution of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> independent (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent). This makes <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> ignored when <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> learning to predict <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> (correctness of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>) from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (hidden state from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This results in following equation, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> share architecture but are trained independently, as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Q}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Q}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in different representational spaces.</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{Q}))\approx\mathcal{A}(\phi^{\prime}(s^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{Q}))\approx\mathcal{A}(\phi^{\prime}(s^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The resulting <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{Q}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{Q}))</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Computing of self-awareness ‣ 3.2 Approximate question-side effect ‣ 3 AQE: assessing question-side effects of hallucination prediction datasets ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation 4</span></a> is defined as AQE. To summarize, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> predicts <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> without using information of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Intuitively, a distinct model <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> predicts whether <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> will succeed on a given question, using only the information from the question. As no information about <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is involved, this setup excludes self-awareness and reflects only question-awareness. Together with <a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 3.2 Approximate question-side effect ‣ 3 AQE: assessing question-side effects of hallucination prediction datasets ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation 2</span></a>, we can derive <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{M}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{M}))</annotation></semantics></math>, the component of self-awareness in the measured hallucination prediction performance: <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{M}))\approx\mathcal{A}(\phi(s))-\mathcal{A}(\phi^{\prime}(s^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\phi(s_{M}))\approx\mathcal{A}(\phi(s))-\mathcal{A}(\phi^{\prime}(s^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, this formulation of AQE holds only under the assumption that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is the hidden state format. When <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is a confidence score, AQE cannot be directly applied because confidence score is a one-dimensional value, which is too saturated to embed high-level information of the question.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:55%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">AQE assessment on datasets. Prediction of <math id="S3.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> from <span id="S3.T2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaMA-3-8B</span>, with <math id="S3.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> from sBERT.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S3.T2.6" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:189.8pt;height:33.1pt;vertical-align:-15.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-116.3pt,20.3pt) scale(0.449245916478251,0.449245916478251) ;">
