<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Experimental detail</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Detail on the three main approaches</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide detailed descriptions of the three main approaches evaluated in <a href="#S5" title="5 Experiment on hallucination prediction approaches ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§5</span></a>.
(1) confidence-based: We adopt a simplified method that takes the top-<math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> softmax probabilities of the first answer token and applies a threshold. <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and the threshold <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> are learnable <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the threshold-based discrimination, we first use the mean value of top-<math id="A5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vocabulary (<math id="A5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{th}</annotation></semantics></math> token confidence in top-<math id="A5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> candidates) and apply the threshold, as depicted in <a href="#A5.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ E.1 Detail on the three main approaches ‣ Appendix E Experimental detail ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation 5</span></a>. Here, the learnable parameter <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi=\{t,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi=\{t,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a threshold (<math id="A5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>) and the number of vocabulary candidates (<math id="A5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>). During the training session, every possible pair of <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and threshold (<math id="A5.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is 1 to 30 in 30 steps, <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is 0 to 0.1 in 3000 steps, total 90K <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> pairs) are measured on the training dataset, and the pair with the highest accuracy is applied to the test session.</p>
<table id="A5.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phi(s)=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}s_{j}\geq t\\
0,&amp;\text{if }\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}s_{j}&lt;t\par\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phi(s)=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}s_{j}\geq t\\
0,&amp;\text{if }\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}s_{j}&lt;t\par\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F8" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:39%;"><img src="2509.15339v1/images/fusion_structure.png" id="A5.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:172/165;" width="172" height="165" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A5.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Structure of aggregation of hidden-state and confidence scores.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(2) hidden-state-based: We employ a 3-layer deep neural network (DNN) structure with dimensions <math id="A5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\rightarrow 100\rightarrow 30\rightarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>100</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>30</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\rightarrow 100\rightarrow 30\rightarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the hidden size. ReLU activation is applied between each layer. The objective function of DNN is binary cross entropy loss <math id="A5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L=-\frac{1}{N}\sum[y\cdot\log(\phi(s))+(1-y)\cdot\log(1-\phi(s))]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=-\frac{1}{N}\sum[y\cdot\log(\phi(s))+(1-y)\cdot\log(1-\phi(s))]</annotation></semantics></math>. DNN (<math id="A5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>) is trained on the dataset while <math id="A5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is frozen. The choice of which layer’s hidden state from <math id="A5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> to use is determined during the training phase, based on the one that achieves the highest validation accuracy.
This approach extends the linear probing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze that DNN emulates the mechanism of the mean threshold approach. The weights of the first layer decide how many candidates to count in, corresponding to the function of <math id="A5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in the threshold-based approach. The second layer decides operations, such as mean or max pooling. DNN structure is a more suitable choice if feature fusion with other data is required.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">(3) aggregation: We utilize the feature fusion of confidence and hidden state. That implies utilizing both top-30 confidence value and <math id="A5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{th}</annotation></semantics></math> hidden state from <math id="A5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> as inputs to DNN. This approach concatenates the confidence scores and hidden state into a single vector, which is then passed to a learnable <math id="A5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. We use a module with dimensions of <math id="A5.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d+n)\rightarrow 100\rightarrow 30\rightarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>100</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>30</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d+n)\rightarrow 100\rightarrow 30\rightarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A5.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed to 30 (<a href="#A5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ E.1 Detail on the three main approaches ‣ Appendix E Experimental detail ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 8</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Experiment pipeline</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First the dataset is divided into <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{train}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{train}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{valid}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{valid}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{test}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{test}</annotation></semantics></math>. We fit <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{train}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{train}</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is frozen. The next step varies between the two types.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Learning-based</h4>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The methods with hidden-state (<span id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Probe</span> and <span id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Probe + Conf</span>) should employ DNN architecture for <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, which need machine learning. In this case, <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is trained on the <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{train}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{train}</annotation></semantics></math> with the objective of BCELoss. We train for 20 epochs to get <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. And from the final checkpoint, we choose the index of the hidden layer of <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> with the best accuracy on the <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{valid}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{valid}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we use this hidden layer index and <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> to test on the <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{test}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{test}</annotation></semantics></math>. We calculate two metrics of accuracy and AUROC. When training, the learning rate is 1e-3, and the optimizer is AdamW.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Threshold-based</h4>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The method that solely relies on confidence score (<span id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Conf</span>) uses a threshold for calibration. Here, learnable <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of top candidate confidence scores (<math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>) and the threshold (<math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>). These two parameters are fitted in <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{train}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{train}</annotation></semantics></math>, without evaluation on <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{valid}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{valid}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We select the <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> that achieves the highest accuracy by performing a grid search over <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> values in the range <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> with 1000 uniformly spaced points, and <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in the range <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,30]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>30</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,30]</annotation></semantics></math> with 3000 uniformly spaced points. And use this <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> to test on the <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{test}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{test}</annotation></semantics></math>. AUROC is measured only with <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, without <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3 </span>AQE of larger model</h3>

<div id="A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide the AQE results of the larger model (<span id="A5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct</span>) on the refined dataset, corresponding to <a href="#A5.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ E.3 AQE of larger model ‣ Appendix E Experimental detail ‣ Quantifying Self-Awareness of Knowledge in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A5.T9.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 9</span>: </span><span id="A5.T9.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">AQE score of dataset and <span id="A5.T9.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaMA3-70B-Instruct</span>. The version (original, type , domain) with the lowest AQE within each dataset is highlighted in <span id="A5.T9.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>.</span></figcaption>
<div id="A5.T9.7" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:46.8pt;vertical-align:-21.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-98.8pt,13.4pt) scale(0.635730717686285,0.635730717686285) ;">
